This whole section of `NFTokenMintMask`s is just adding or removing one bit in a previously defined mask.  Repeating the names of all the flags in every definition seems kind of error prone to me.  Consider changing all of the definitions to simply build on a previously defined `NFTokenMintMask`.  It might look like this:
```
constexpr std::uint32_t const tfNFTokenMintMask =
    ~(tfUniversal | tfBurnable | tfOnlyXRP | tfTransferable);

constexpr std::uint32_t const tfNFTokenMintOldMask =
    ~( ~tfNFTokenMintMask | tfTrustLine);

// if featureDynamicNFT enabled then new flag allowing mutable URI available.
constexpr std::uint32_t const tfNFTokenMintOldMaskWithMutable =
    ~( ~tfNFTokenMintOldMask | tfMutable);

constexpr std::uint32_t const tfNFTokenMintMaskWithMutable =
    ~( ~tfNFTokenMintMask | tfMutable);
```
That said, what you have here works.  So no change is required.

If you think you would like to make the change you are welcome to do so by cherry-picking commit 8aec32c0b8d814ce4504c6353816aab8764a8003 in branch https://github.com/scottschurr/rippled/commits/tequ-featureDynamicNFT/.  That commit makes the suggested changes and adds some whitespace changes to tidy up the section of the file that isn't enforced by clang-format.

But that's purely at your discretion.

I don't think this test actually tests what you think it is testing.  Because you are using `nftId_not_modifiable`, `preclaim()` bails before you get to the invalid minter check.  You can help prevent some mistakes like this by limiting the scope of locals using braces.  You've used that technique in other parts of this test.  I think it would be good to apply that technique to this section as well.

When I applied that technique in this section, here is what the results looked like:
```
